
🛠️ Act III: The Execution Diary (The Technical Codebase)

Here is the step-by-step programming logic we used to move data out of raw storage, through dbt models, and straight into the Power BI semantic layer.

🥉 Phase 1: Ingestion & Staging (The Bronze Layer SQL)

To protect data integrity, the staging files do not modify the raw data. They simply clean up column naming conventions, enforce clean data types, and prepare the tables for modeling.
sql
-- file: models/staging/stg_physical_baselines.sql
WITH raw_baselines AS (
    SELECT 
        UPPER(TRIM(store_id)) AS store_id,
        UPPER(TRIM(sku_id))   AS product_id,
        CAST(physical_baseline_qty AS INT) AS baseline_qty,
        CAST(audit_verified_date AS DATE)  AS audit_date
    FROM {{ source('phantom_raw', 'stg_physical_baselines') }}
)
SELECT * FROM raw_baselines;
Use code with caution.

🥈 Phase 2: Structural Enrichment (The Silver Layer SQL)

This is where the heavy analytical lifting happens. We wrote a Unified Chronology Timeline Spine to ensure that even if a store had no sales or shipments on a specific day, the timeline remains unbroken. We then computed windowed running totals using SQL analytical functions.
sql
-- file: models/intermediate/int_silver_inventory_reconciliation.sql
WITH calendar_spine AS (
    -- Generates a seamless date backbone so there are zero calendar gaps
    SELECT DISTINCT date_day FROM {{ ref('dim_calendar') }}
),

store_product_matrix AS (
    -- Cross-joins stores and products to ensure absolute timeline coverage
    SELECT s.store_key, p.product_key, c.date_day
    FROM {{ ref('dim_stores') }} s
    CROSS JOIN {{ ref('dim_products') }} p
    CROSS JOIN calendar_spine c
),

running_inventory_metrics AS (
    SELECT 
        matrix.store_key,
        matrix.product_key,
        matrix.date_day,
        COALESCE(b.baseline_qty, 0) AS initial_baseline,
        
        -- Calculates windowed running totals for inventory additions
        SUM(COALESCE(rcv.received_qty, 0)) OVER (
            PARTITION BY matrix.store_key, matrix.product_key 
            ORDER BY matrix.date_day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
        ) AS running_received_qty,

        -- Calculates windowed running totals for inventory subtractions
        SUM(COALESCE(sales.sales_qty, 0)) OVER (
            PARTITION BY matrix.store_key, matrix.product_key 
            ORDER BY matrix.date_day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
        ) AS running_sales_qty
        
    FROM store_product_matrix matrix
    LEFT JOIN {{ ref('stg_physical_baselines') }} b 
        ON matrix.store_key = b.store_id AND matrix.product_key = b.product_key AND matrix.date_day = b.audit_date
    LEFT JOIN {{ ref('stg_inbound_receiving') }} rcv 
        ON matrix.store_key = rcv.store_id AND matrix.product_key = rcv.product_key AND matrix.date_day = rcv.received_date
    LEFT JOIN {{ ref('stg_pos_sales') }} sales 
        ON matrix.store_key = sales.store_id AND matrix.product_key = sales.product_key AND matrix.date_day = sales.sales_date
)

SELECT 
    store_key,
    product_key,
    date_day,
    -- Expected Inventory Formula: Baseline + what came in - what went out
    (initial_baseline + running_received_qty - running_sales_qty) AS expected_inventory_qty
FROM running_inventory_metrics;
Use code with caution.

🥇 Phase 3: Dimensional Optimization & Modeling (The Gold Layer SQL)

To serve Power BI, we optimized the database structure into a high-performance Star Schema. This separates descriptors (Dimensions) from math (Facts) to completely eliminate visual lag.

1. Designing the Dimensions (dim_products.sql)

sql
-- file: models/marts/dim_products.sql
SELECT 
    -- Generates a unique, clean hash key for fast database indexing
    MD5(UPPER(TRIM(sku_id))) AS product_key,
    sku_id                  AS product_sku,
    COALESCE(product_name, 'Unknown Item') AS product_name,
    COALESCE(category, 'General Inventory') AS product_category,
    unit_price              AS product_retail_price
FROM {{ ref('stg_products_raw') }};
Use code with caution.

2. Designing the Core Fact Table (fact_inventory_snapshot.sql)

sql
-- file: models/marts/fact_inventory_snapshot.sql
SELECT 
    MD5(store_id)   AS store_key,
    MD5(product_id) AS product_key,
    date_day        AS snapshot_date,
    expected_inventory_qty,
    
    -- Anomaly Variance Metric tracking logic
    CASE 
        WHEN expected_inventory_qty < 0 THEN 'Negative Stock Warning'
        WHEN expected_inventory_qty = 0 THEN 'Stockout Outage'
        ELSE 'Healthy Allocation'
    END AS operational_status
FROM {{ ref('int_silver_inventory_reconciliation') }};
Use code with caution.

📊 Phase 4: Connecting the Semantic Layer (Power BI Integration)

Once the Gold layer tables were generated inside Snowflake, we exposed them directly to the Power BI Semantic Layer.
• The Star Schema Connection: Inside the Power BI modeling window, we dragged and dropped connections to form a perfect star layout—linking the product_key, store_key, and date_day fields from our dimension tables straight into the centered fact_inventory_snapshot table using 1-to-many (1:*) relationships.
• DAX Business Measures: Instead of performing sluggish column calculations, we built fast, dynamic DAX measures directly inside the semantic layer to calculate inventory exceptions on the fly:
dax
-- DAX Measure inside Power BI for real-time Alert Tracking
Inventory Anomaly Count = 
CALCULATE(
    COUNTROWS(fact_inventory_snapshot), 
    fact_inventory_snapshot[operational_status] = "Negative Stock Warning"
)
